runs/
outputs/
data/standings/inbox/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.env
.DS_Store

# transient per-slate working inputs (bundle, salaries, entries); the
# durable record is data/archive/<date>/, not data/slates/
data/slates/*/

# stray root-level bundle (belongs in data/slates/<date>/)
/slate_bundle.json

# ad-hoc session scratch
_scratch_*/

# anchor-splice backups. `.claude/rules/board.md` requires a `.bak` beside the
# target on every board or changelog splice, so every such session used to leave
# an untracked file that reads as foreign dirt at the next session's start. The
# backup is a within-session safety net; git history is the durable one.
*.md.bak

# generate-lineups iteration scratch (build scripts, review HTML)
skills/generate-lineups-workspace/

# stray loose feed/bundle files at the slates root (per-slate dirs are ignored
# above). Both shapes appear: an underscore-prefixed scratch name and a
# date-prefixed raw feed dump, e.g. 2026-07-27_lineups_feed_raw.json.
data/slates/*.json

# stray root-level DK downloads. The authoritative copies live in
# data/slates/<date>/ during a build and data/archive/<date>/ after it; a root
# copy is a leftover and is never what a build reads.
/DKEntries.csv
/DKSalaries.csv
/DKEntries*.csv
/DKSalaries*.csv

# audit stdout capture
/audit_out.log
audit_out.log
# Every session that redirects an audit picks its own name; the two above only
# covered one. A stray audit log in the repo root reads as foreign dirt at the
# next session's start and costs someone a paragraph deciding whose it is.
_audit.log
*_audit.log
audit*.log

# zero-byte DK exports quarantined by the pull step; they are failed pulls, not
# evidence, and the contest stays on the awaiting list until a real pull lands
data/standings/failed_pulls/

# superseded worklists the sandbox could not delete; status lives in
# CONTESTS_AWAITING_STANDINGS.md, which is tracked
data/standings/PENDING_MINE_*.md

# R315(b), NOT DONE and deliberately so. `standings_pulls_<date>.html` (the
# dated click-through list `awaiting_standings.py` writes beside the checklist)
# looks like the PENDING_MINE_* class above and was about to be ignored on that
# reasoning. It is not that class: SEVEN of them are already TRACKED
# (2026-08-03, -04, -08, -10, -20, -22, -28), so this project has been treating
# them as history, and `git ls-files -i -c` is what said so. Ignoring the
# pattern would have left seven files in the index that git no longer watches,
# which is worse than either state. Whether the series should be tracked at all
# is ARCHIVE's call and it is filed as R315(b); until then the newest one is
# ordinary uncommitted ARCHIVE work, not residue.

# vendored interpreter state: a local scipy install and its wheel cache, plus the
# scratch dir. Environment, never history. `export PYTHONPATH=$PWD/.pylibs` is what
# makes scipy.optimize.milp importable without a network install (2026-08-03).
.pylibs/
.wheels/
.tmp/

# raw DK zips whose CSV is already mined into data/archive/. The extracted CSV is
# the durable record and it is tracked; the zip is the same bytes a second time.
# Moved here rather than deleted because the sandbox cannot delete (2026-08-03).
data/standings/processed_zips/

# the same rule one directory over (2026-08-29). Zips also land in
# data/archive/<date>/ beside the CSV they were mined from, and there they read as
# untracked dirt on every `git status` while being the same bytes the tracked CSV
# already holds. Verified before adding: all 17 present on this date had their
# mined CSV alongside, so nothing unmined is being hidden. If a zip ever arrives
# WITHOUT its CSV it is an unmined pull, and the awaiting-standings checklist is
# what reports that, not git.
data/archive/**/*.zip

# root-level tarball residue from the disk-to-container bridge, same class as
# _stage_repo*.tar.gz below but named per session (_changes, _final_0827, ...).
# Environment, never history.
/_*.tar.gz

# a timestamped .bak beside a reference file is a session's own safety copy; the
# tracked file is the record.
data/reference/*.bak.json
# R315(a). The comment above says "a timestamped .bak beside a reference file",
# and the pattern under it only covered `.bak.json`. A session's CSV safety
# copy therefore read as untracked dirt on every scan while the sentence above
# said it should not: `fangraphs_season_pitching.csv.qual-y-backup-20260830`
# sat in `git status` for five days for exactly that reason. Two patterns, both
# narrowed to `data/reference/` and both requiring a suffix AFTER a real
# extension, so a genuine reference file can never match. Verified with
# `git ls-files -i -c --exclude-standard` before adding: these two hide ZERO
# tracked files. The same check is why the standings_pulls note below says NOT
# DONE -- that pattern hid seven.
data/reference/*.csv.*backup*
data/reference/*.csv.bak*

# files a session finished with and could not delete: the sandbox denies rm on the
# mount. Ben empties this folder; nothing here is history and nothing reads it.
_to_delete/

# multi-session claims: live state, never history (keep the dir itself)
claims/*
!claims/.gitkeep

# BUILD byproduct cache (lineups_from_paste); untracked by design (R-merge 2026-08-04)
data/reference/paste_cache/

# residue of the disk-to-container tarball bridge in docs/cowork_sync_protocol.md.
# Environment, never history; the mount cannot delete it, so it is ignored instead
# of swept, and it stops reading as unexplained dirt at session start (R107(b)).
_stage_repo*.tar.gz

# R152 split-gate state: per-class results for a gate run across several calls.
# Live session state, never history -- the verdict is the audit's output, and a
# unit record is only evidence about the tree fingerprint stamped on it.
.audit_gate/

# Portable verified engine environment and local lint cache.
.venv/
.ruff_cache/

# R338 step 7. The thirteenth edition left twenty run artifacts under
# docs/greenfield/2026-09-09/ and they are EVIDENCE, not source. Two are large
# enough to change what this repo is (baseline_inventory.json 1.2MB and
# raw_artifact_hashes.json 1.3MB, each a machine-generated snapshot of a tree
# git already stores) and the two JUnit XMLs are one run's console output.
#
# SEVEN land, by explicit path, because they are what a reader needs to check
# the edition's claims rather than take them: changed_files.json (what it
# touched), final_acceptance.json and final_verification.json (what it
# measured), implementation.patch (the diff itself, which is how the R55 test
# was restored verbatim), the two fixture CSVs, and test_artifact_incident.json
# -- the last added to the entry's list on purpose, because R274's rider CITES
# it as the evidence for the class repair (4) closes, and a citation to an
# untracked file is a dead citation.
#
# The other fifteen are named ONE BY ONE rather than hidden behind a directory
# glob. A glob would silently swallow the next edition's evidence too; a named
# list means a new file there reads as untracked dirt and gets a decision.
docs/greenfield/*/baseline_benchmarks.json
docs/greenfield/*/baseline_inventory.json
docs/greenfield/*/baseline_probes.json
docs/greenfield/*/baseline-static.json
docs/greenfield/*/baseline-tests.xml
docs/greenfield/*/final_pytest.log
docs/greenfield/*/final_pytest.xml
docs/greenfield/*/implemented_benchmarks.json
docs/greenfield/*/isolated_test_preservation.json
docs/greenfield/*/legacy_static_audit.json
docs/greenfield/*/offline_demo_result.json
docs/greenfield/*/raw_artifact_hashes.json
docs/greenfield/*/raw_preservation_verification.json
docs/greenfield/*/safe_recovery_verification.json
docs/greenfield/*/schema_inventory.json

# Claude Code (R301, 2026-09-15). The shared settings, hooks, rules and skills
# under .claude/ are tracked; the personal overlay and the per-host notes file
# are this machine's and never history.
.claude/settings.local.json
CLAUDE.local.md
